# --- playlist-generator: per-stage pipeline drip (TRIAGE-2026-09-03) ---
# REPLACES the single `POST /sync/full-pipeline` SSE stream, which could not
# succeed. One pass needs 3+ days: the Last.fm track stage alone measured
# ~1,900 tracks/h (10,500 -> 17,400 of 144,756 between 11:06 and 14:44 on
# 2026-09-03), so ~67h remained for that ONE stage. The old line ran every 6h
# with --max-age-min 1440, so it was judged stale after 24h and paged hourly
# forever while working correctly, and `Stream closed without a completion
# signal` recurred from 2026-08-24 onward at 100min / 10.7h / 21h+ durations.
#
# Bounded batches are the fix: each run finishes in minutes, the freshness clock
# advances every run, and the backlog drains over days. Same shape as
# lidarr_backlog_drip.py and album_art.py --limit.
#
# ONLY the I/O-bound stages are scheduled, and that distinction is measured:
# the backend is a single uvicorn worker, so
#   * I/O-bound stages (lastfm-*, release-dates) await on HTTP and leave the
#     event loop free -- playlist-generator stayed HEALTHY through a 22h Last.fm
#     run;
#   * CPU-bound derived stages (embeddings, profiles, clusters, banger-flags,
#     genre-manifold, audio) peg a core inside the request handler and block
#     /health completely -- one banger-flags run drove the container to
#     `unhealthy streak=23` at CPU=101%. Scheduling those would manufacture the
#     alert noise this change exists to remove, so they stay MANUAL via
#     `python scripts/playlist_sync_stage.py --stage <name>` until the backend
#     runs them off the event loop. See TRIAGE-2026-09-03.md.
#
# All three share one lock so no two stages ever overlap.
# Every 30 min -- the long pole. Two rates were measured: ~1,900 tracks/h in
# the long monolithic run, but only ~770/h for a fresh 20-track batch (93.7s),
# so per-run overhead is real. 300/batch sized off the SLOWER number keeps a
# run inside the 30min interval; if one overruns, flock -n skips the next tick
# harmlessly and the 180min window absorbs it. ~14,400 tracks/day either way.
*/30 * * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name playlist-lastfm-tracks --lock /tmp/nas-playlist-stage.lock --max-age-min 180 -- python scripts/playlist_sync_stage.py --stage lastfm-tracks --limit 300 >> logs/playlist_sync.log 2>&1"
